answer:
The cargo of the freighter that left aboard was that enormous relief shipment the U.S. government put out‑of‑quarantine to the war‑torn waters of the Mediterranean in the spring of 1943.  
After the calorie–stuff that filled the ship’s holds (evaporated milk, citrus juice, margarine, dried apples and wheat flour), the other $12 \text{ million}$ pounds of aid were in the form of **malaria prophylaxis tablets – quinine‑sulfate**.

Each tablet weighed 0.005 lb (2.3 g).  The difference between the total cargo weight (12 000 000 lb) and the individual foodstuffs listed (about 9 964 000 lb, including the 20 000 pairs of children’s shoes) left:

\[
12\,000\,000\;\text{lb} \;\;-\;\; 9\,964\,000\;\text{lb}
\;=\; 2\,036\,000\;\text{lb}
\]

Dividing by the weight of a single tablet gives the number of tablets carried:

\[
\frac{2\,036\,000\;\text{lb}}{0.005\;\text{lb/tablet}}
\;=\; 407\,200\,000\;\text{tablets}
\]

So the freighter carried **407 million quinine‑sulfate tablets** as part of its relief cargo.

